Yes. This one is different from P2P Edge because this is using Spring Retry's @Retryable, not Reactor's Retry.

For this method, I would not try to force the Reactor approach. Since @Retryable is already managing the retries, the cleanest solution is to use a Spring Retry RetryListener.

Recommended flow

processValidZipCodes()
        ↓
DataIntegrityViolationException
        ↓
@Retryable
        ↓
RetryListener → log exception + retry count
        ↓
Retry
        ↓
Exception again
        ↓
RetryListener → log exception + retry count
        ↓
Retry
        ↓
Exception again
        ↓
Retry exhausted
        ↓
RetryListener → log final exception

1. Create a RetryListener

For example, create:

config/
   RetryLoggingListener.java

@Slf4j
@Component
public class RetryLoggingListener implements RetryListener {

    @Override
    public <T, E extends Throwable> boolean open(
            RetryContext context,
            RetryCallback<T, E> callback) {
        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public <T, E extends Throwable> void onError(
            RetryContext context,
            RetryCallback<T, E> callback,
            Throwable throwable) {

        log.error(
            "Retry attempt {} failed. Exception: {} - {}",
            context.getRetryCount(),
            throwable.getClass().getSimpleName(),
            throwable.getMessage(),
            throwable
        );
    }

    @Override
    public <T, E extends Throwable> void close(
            RetryContext context,
            RetryCallback<T, E> callback,
            Throwable throwable) {

        if (throwable != null) {
            log.error(
                "Retry exhausted after {} attempts. Final exception: {} - {}",
                context.getRetryCount(),
                throwable.getClass().getSimpleName(),
                throwable.getMessage(),
                throwable
            );
        }
    }
}

The important method here is:

onError(...)

It gets called whenever an attempt fails, so you'll get logging for each retry.


---

2. Tell @Retryable to use the listener

You can reference the listener using listeners:

@Retryable(
    retryFor = {DataIntegrityViolationException.class},
    maxAttempts = 3,
    backoff = @Backoff(
        delay = 100,
        maxDelay = 400,
        random = true
    ),
    listeners = {"retryLoggingListener"}
)

Your method then becomes:

@Override
@Transactional
@Retryable(
    retryFor = {DataIntegrityViolationException.class},
    maxAttempts = 3,
    backoff = @Backoff(
        delay = 100,
        maxDelay = 400,
        random = true
    ),
    listeners = {"retryLoggingListener"}
)
public void processValidZipCodes(
        Set<Long> validZipCodes,
        String customerId,
        String facilityId,
        String serviceLevel) {

    var zipCodeUpsertSummary =
            getZipCodeUpsertSummary(
                    validZipCodes,
                    customerId,
                    facilityId);

    var zipCodesToInsert =
            zipCodeUpsertSummary.getZipCodesToInsert()
                    .stream()
                    .map(zipCode ->
                            constructZipCodeEntity(
                                    serviceLevel,
                                    customerId,
                                    facilityId,
                                    zipCode))
                    .toList();

    if (!zipCodesToInsert.isEmpty()) {
        zipcodePreferenceRepository.saveAll(zipCodesToInsert);
    }

    if (!zipCodeUpsertSummary.getZipCodesToUpdateTrue().isEmpty()) {
        updateZipCodePreference(
                customerId,
                facilityId,
                zipCodeUpsertSummary.getZipCodesToUpdateTrue(),
                serviceLevel,
                Boolean.TRUE);
    }

    if (!zipCodeUpsertSummary.getZipCodesToUpdateFalse().isEmpty()) {
        updateZipCodePreference(
                customerId,
                facilityId,
                zipCodeUpsertSummary.getZipCodesToUpdateFalse(),
                serviceLevel,
                Boolean.FALSE);
    }

    zipcodePreferenceRepository.flush();
}

One important consideration

Before creating a global listener, check whether this application already has a RetryListener somewhere.

Search the project for:

RetryListener

and:

RetryConfigurer

If there is already a common listener/configuration, extend that instead of creating another one.

Also, because this retry is specifically for:

DataIntegrityViolationException

your listener will only be involved when that retryable exception occurs. You don't need to change the exception list.

Expected logs

With maxAttempts = 3, you'd get something similar to:

Retry attempt 1 failed.
Exception: DataIntegrityViolationException - ...

then:

Retry attempt 2 failed.
Exception: DataIntegrityViolationException - ...

and finally:

Retry exhausted after 3 attempts.
Final exception: DataIntegrityViolationException - ...

One thing I'd verify before you implement this: the exact Spring Retry version in this project, because listener registration and @Retryable(listeners = ...) behavior can vary slightly between versions. If you share the project's existing retry dependencies (spring-retry version / build.gradle or pom.xml), I can give you the exact implementation for this service.